Here is a function in java which takes a string and a boolean as input and returns the modified string based on the boolean value.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(switchCaseAndPossiblyReverse("Hello World!", true));
        System.out.println(switchCaseAndPossiblyReverse("Hello World!", false));
        System.out.println(switchCaseAndPossiblyReverse("java Programming!", true));
        System.out.println(switchCaseAndPossiblyReverse("java Programming!", false));

        // Edge case: Empty String
        System.out.println(switchCaseAndPossiblyReverse("", true));
        // Edge case: String with spaces only
        System.out.println(switchCaseAndPossiblyReverse("   ", true));
        // Edge case: String with special characters
        System.out.println(switchCaseAndPossiblyReverse("@#$$%", true));
        // Edge case: String with numbers
        System.out.println(switchCaseAndPossiblyReverse("12345", true));

    }

    public static String switchCaseAndPossiblyReverse(String str, boolean reverse) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        // Change case for each character
        for (char c : str.toCharArray()) {
            if (Character.isUpperCase(c)) {
                sb.append(Character.toLowerCase(c));
            } else {
                sb.append(Character.toUpperCase(c));
            }
        }
        if (reverse) {
            // Split by words and reverse order
            String[] words = sb.toString().split(" ");
            sb.setLength(0);
            for (int i = words.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
                sb.append(words[i]);
                if (i != 0) {
                    sb.append(" ");
                }
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

